{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1836 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "07fc7ecb9efb9bfc0eed41e8ccfd43b3c200b41d127a7e2dd827564226f9c2ee" :edb/db-before-t 1835 :edb/db-after-t 1836 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535148 50 #inst "2026-10-13T01:02:49.680Z" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052019 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052020 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052021 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475580 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475615 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475631 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052018 1078 #edb/ref 17592186475615 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052888 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052889 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052890 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052891 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052892 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475610 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475732 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475756 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475760 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475789 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186052887 1078 #edb/ref 17592186475610 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054349 1074 "The polynomial $1+2x^2 - 8x$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $2$, so it is quadratic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quadratic trinomial." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054349 1074 "The polynomial $1+2x^2 - 8x$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $2,$ so it is quadratic. \n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quadratic trinomial." 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054350 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The sum of $x^2+2$ and $3x$ is a trinomial.\n2. The sum of $5x^2+4x$ and $-4x$ is a trinomial.\n3. The sum of a monomial and a binomial is **always** a trinomial." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054350 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The sum of $x^2+2$ and $3x$ is a trinomial.\n\nII. The sum of $5x^2+4x$ and $-4x$ is a trinomial.\n\nIII. The sum of a monomial and a binomial is **always** a trinomial." 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054350 1074 "Let's analyze each statement, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n(x^2+2) + (3x) = x^2+3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a trinomial.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n(5x^2+4x) + (-4x) = 5x^2,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a trinomial (it's a monomial).\n\n- Statement III is false. The sum of a monomial and a binomial *could be* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement I).\n\nHowever, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement II).\n\nSo, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054350 1074 "Let's analyze each statement, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n$(x^2+2) + (3x) = x^2+3x+2$,\nwhich is a trinomial.\n- Statement II is false. We have\n$(5x^2+4x) + (-4x) = 5x^2$,\nwhich is *not* a trinomial (it's a monomial).\n- Statement III is false. The sum of a monomial and a binomial *could be* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement I).However, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement II).So, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054371 1074 "The number $7$ is multiplied by itself $6$ times, so we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7\\times 7}_{\\large{\\color{blue}6} \\textrm{ copies of } 7} = 7^{\\color{blue}6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $7^6.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054371 1074 "The number $7$ is multiplied by itself $6$ times, so we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7 \\times 7\\times 7}_{\\large{\\color{blue}6} \\textrm{copies of} 7} = 7^{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $7^6$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054372 1071 "Evaluate $3^4$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054372 1071 "Evaluate $3^4.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054372 1074 "The expression $3^4$ means $3$ multiplied by itself $4$ times. So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{4} &= (3 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3) \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 81\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054372 1074 "The expression $3^4$ means $3$ multiplied by itself $4$ times. So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4} & =(3\\times 3)\\times (3\\times 3) \\\\  & =9\\times 9 \\\\  & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054373 1071 "Evaluate $9^1$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054373 1071 "Evaluate $9^1.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054373 1074 "Any number raised to the first power is itself. So $9^1 = 9$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186054373 1074 "Any number raised to the first power is itself. So $9^1 = 9.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054710 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475965 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054710 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475967 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054711 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475918 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054711 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475973 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475925 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475927 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475940 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475954 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475956 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475926 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475960 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054878 1050 420.0 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186054924 1050 420.0 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055097 1071 "$3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3=$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055097 1071 "What is $3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186055097 1074 "The number $3$ is multiplied by itself $5$ times, so we have\n\n$$\n{{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{5\\text{ copies of }3}={3}^{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is ${3}^{5}.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055275 1071 "$(-\\frac{2}{3})\\times (-\\frac{2}{3})\\times (-\\frac{2}{3})\\times (-\\frac{2}{3})=$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055275 1071 "What is $(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}) \\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}) \\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186055275 1074 "The number $(-\\frac{2}{3})$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times, so we have\n\n$$\n{{(-\\frac{2}{3})\\times (-\\frac{2}{3})\\times (-\\frac{2}{3})\\times (-\\frac{2}{3})}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies of }(-\\frac{2}{3})}={(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is ${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055997 1064 "Polynomials with one, two, or three terms have special names.\n\n- A polynomial with one term is called a **monomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\n1, \\qquad y,\t\\qquad\t2x^3,\t\\qquad\t5a^{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following expressions are not monomials because they can't be called polynomials:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y},\t\\qquad\t5t^{-1},\t\\qquad \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad \t2z^{1/4}\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with two terms is called a **binomial**. For example:\n\n$$\nx+3,\t\\qquad\t7n^2 +n,\t\\qquad\tp^{32} -1\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with three terms is called a **trinomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\nz^2 -z +1,\t\\qquad\tx^3 -2x -1,\t\\qquad\t2c^{4} - 3c^{3} +4c^2\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055997 1064 "Polynomials with one, two, or three terms have special names.\n\n- A polynomial with one term is called a **monomial**. For example:\n$1, \\qquad y, \\qquad 2x^3, \\qquad 5a^{4}$\nHowever, the following expressions are not monomials because they can't be called polynomials:\n$\\dfrac{1}{y}, \\qquad 5t^{-1}, \\qquad \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 2z^{1/4}$\n- A polynomial with two terms is called a **binomial**. For example:\n$x+3, \\qquad 7n^2 +n, \\qquad p^{32} -1$\n- A polynomial with three terms is called a **trinomial**. For example:\n$z^2 -z +1, \\qquad x^3 -2x -1, \\qquad 2c^{4} - 3c^{3} +4c^2$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186055998 1064 "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name. These are shown in the table below.\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |\n\nWe can combine these with the names we learned earlier. \n\nFor example:\n\n- $2x+1$ is a *linear binomial* because its degree equals $1,$ and it has $2$ terms.\n\n- $x^2$ is a *quadratic monomial* because its degree equals $2,$ and it has $1$ term.\n\n- $2x^3+x+1$ is a *cubic trinomial* because its degree equals $3,$ and it has $3$ terms.\n\nIf a polynomial contains more than three terms, then we refer to it by its degree only.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $x^3+2x^2+x-1$ is a *cubic polynomial.*\n\n- $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ is a *quartic polynomial.*" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186055998 1064 "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name. These are shown in the table below.\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |\n\nWe can combine these with the names we learned earlier.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $2x+1$ is a *linear binomial* because its degree equals $1$, and it has $2$ terms.\n- $x^2$ is a *quadratic monomial* because its degree equals $2$, and it has $1$ term.\n- $2x^3+x+1$ is a *cubic trinomial* because its degree equals $3$, and it has $3$ terms.\n\nIf a polynomial contains more than three terms, then we refer to it by its degree only.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $x^3+2x^2+x-1$ is a *cubic polynomial.*\n\n- $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ is a *quartic polynomial.*" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186056006 1064 "We've already seen that $5^2$ means $5\\cdot 5$, and that $5^3$ means $5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5$. We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n\n$$\n5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{copies of} 5}\n$$\n\nThe **exponent** $({\\color{blue}7})$ says how many times the **base** $(5)$ is multiplied by itself.\n\nRecall that $5^2$ is said as \"five squared\" and that $5^3$ is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say $5^7$ in several different ways:\n\n- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five to the power seven.\"\n\nRaising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, $5^{\\color{blue}1}$ means one copy of $5$, so\n\n$$\n5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186056006 1064 "We've already seen that $5^2$ means $5\\cdot 5,$ and that $5^3$ means $5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5.$ We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n\n$$\n5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{ copies of } 5}.\n$$\n\nThe **exponent** $({\\color{blue}7})$ says how many times the **base** $(5)$ is multiplied by itself.\n\nRecall that $5^2$ is said as \"five squared\" and that $5^3$ is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say $5^7$  in several different ways:\n\n- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n\n- \"five to the power seven.\"\n\nRaising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, $5^{\\color{blue}1}$ means one copy of $5,$ so $5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186061743 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061747 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064082 1071 "What is $9^0$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064082 1071 "What is $9^0?$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064082 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1$. So,\n$9^0=1$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064082 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n9^0=1.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064083 1071 "Calculate the value of $(-32\\,000)^0$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064083 1071 "Calculate the value of $(-32\\,000)^0.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064083 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1$. So,\n\n$$\n(-32\\,000)^0=1\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064083 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n(-32\\,000)^0=1.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064084 1071 "What is the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^0$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064084 1071 "What is the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^0?$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064084 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1$. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^0=1\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064084 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^0=1.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064085 1071 "Find the value of $(-0.693)^0$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064085 1071 "Find the value of $(-0.693)^0.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064085 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1$. So,\n\n$$\n(-0.693)^0=1\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064085 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n(-0.693)^0=1.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475931 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475962 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064222 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475913 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064222 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475986 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475911 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475952 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475910 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475930 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064414 1050 420.0 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064611 1071 "Calculate the value of $(\\frac{9}{8})^{0}$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186064611 1071 "Calculate the value of ${(\\frac{9}{8})}^{0}.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186064611 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{(\\frac{9}{8})}^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186065360 1064 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1$. For example,\n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1$. First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{4} &= 16 \\\\\n2^{3} &= 8 \\\\\n2^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n2^{1} &= 2 \\\\\n2^{0} &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186065360 1064 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ For example, \n\n$$\n2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.\n$$\n\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that $2^0 = 1.$ First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{0}}  = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives $1.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186118616 1078 #edb/ref 17592186118619 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186118898 1078 #edb/ref 17592186118902 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186119524 1071 "What is $\\sqrt[3]{9^3}$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186119524 1071 "What is $\\sqrt[3]{9^3}?$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186119525 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{64}}$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186119525 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{64}}.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186119525 1074 "First, notice that the numbers $1$ and $64$ are both perfect cubes.\n\nSo, to determine the value of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{64}}\n$$\n\nwe express\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{64}\n$$\n\nas a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1^{3}}{4^{3}}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}{4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{(\\frac{1}{4})^{3}} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186119525 1074 "First, notice that the numbers $1$ and $64$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{64}},$ we express $\\dfrac{1}{64}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1}{4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186119526 1071 "Calculate $\\sqrt[3]{-125}$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186119526 1071 "Calculate $\\sqrt[3]{-125}.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186119