ce\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^3 = {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2} = 8\n$$\n\nSo, we write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSome basic cube roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[3]{0} &= & \\sqrt[3]{0^{3}}|= & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1}|= & \\sqrt[3]{1^{3}}|= & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8}|= & \\sqrt[3]{2^{3}}|= & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{27}|= & \\sqrt[3]{3^{3}}|= & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{64}|= & \\sqrt[3]{4^{3}}|= & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{125}|= & \\sqrt[3]{5^{3}}|= & 5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1000}|= & \\sqrt[3]{10^{3}}|= & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA **perfect cube** is a number whose cube root is a whole number. So, the numbers\n\n$0,\\qquad 1,\\qquad 8,\\qquad 27,\\qquad 64,\\qquad 125, \\qquad 1\\,000$\nare all perfect cubes. You need to remember them." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186121142 1064 "Finding the **cube root** of a number is the reverse of cubing a number. We express the cube root using the **radical symbol** $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}.$\n\nFor example, the \"cube root of $8$\" equals $\\color{blue}2$ since \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^3 = {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, we write $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}.$\n\nSome basic cube roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{0} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{0}^{3}} & = & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{1}^{3}} & = & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{2}^{3}} & = & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{3}^{3}} & = & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{4}^{3}} & = & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}} & = & 5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{10}^{3}} & = & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA **perfect cube** is a number whose cube root is a whole number. So, the numbers \n\n$$\n0,\\qquad 1,\\qquad  8,\\qquad  27,\\qquad  64,\\qquad 125, \\qquad 1\\,000\n$$\n\n are all perfect cubes. You need to remember them." 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186121143 1064 "Unlike the square root, it *is* possible to find the cube root of a negative number.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{-8}={\\color{blue}{-2}}\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n-8 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})^3 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\n$$\n\nSome basic cube roots of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[3]{-1} &= & \\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{3}}|= & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-8}|= & \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}}|= & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-27}|= & \\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}}|= & -3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-64}|= & \\sqrt[3]{(-4)^{3}}|= & -4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-125}|= & \\sqrt[3]{(-5)^{3}}|= & -5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-1000}|= & \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}}|= & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the numbers\n\n$$\n-1,\\qquad -8,\\qquad -27,\\qquad -64,\\qquad -125, \\qquad -1\\,000\n$$\n\nare all perfect cubes." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186121143 1064 "Unlike the square root, it *is* possible to find the cube root of a negative number.\n\nFor example, $\\sqrt[3]{-8}={\\color{blue}{-2}}$ because\n\n$$\n-8 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})^3 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}}).\n$$\n\nSome basic cube roots of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{3}} & = & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}} & = & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}} & = & -3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-4)^{3}} & = & -4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-5)^{3}} & = & -5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}} & = & -10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the numbers \n\n$$\n-1,\\qquad  -8,\\qquad  -27,\\qquad  -64,\\qquad -125, \\qquad -1\\,000\n$$\n\nare all perfect cubes." 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186121144 1064 "Let's calculate $(\\sqrt[3]{64})^3$ by performing each operation one step at a time:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\color{blue}64}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\sqrt[3]{{\\color{red}4}^3}\\right)^3 = {\\color{red}4}^3 = {\\color{blue}64