g)\n\nNow, we can pile them up in the form of a cube where each side is equal to $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123165 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123167 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123168 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186123169 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475603 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475669 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475709 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186123164 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475720 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186123164 1078 #edb/ref 17592186475720 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186124457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475943 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186124535 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475976 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186124551 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475939 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186124551 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475978 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186124551 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475980 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186125060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122960 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186125094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186125094 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186125094 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186125094 1074 "Since the matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries of the main diagonal. Therefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=12$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=12.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{12}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=12$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{12})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-6}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-6)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-6)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]-(-6)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 18 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{12})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-6})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186125132 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186125132 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186127171 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127172 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186127171 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127173 13194139535148 17592186045417