13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129342 1050 780.0 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129615 1071 "![](images/7e/7e979ac75636efa00cbc32ee92eff1a5fc1da3cea19378fbf5a39a5cc954ff45.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y = x^{2}$ and lines $y = 7x$, $y = x + 12$, as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x, y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186129615 1071 "![](images/7e/7e979ac75636efa00cbc32ee92eff1a5fc1da3cea19378fbf5a39a5cc954ff45.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and lines $y=7x,$ $y=x+12,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129615 1074 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/ee6fd6a0b89a879d5803a98fda7e80a93926566995bea5f47a05aa7ab2986375.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 7x\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{7x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+12}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129616 1071 "${∬}_{|x|+|y|\\le 1}\\,\\,{e}^{x+y}\\,dA=$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129616 1071 "What is $∬_(\\begin{vmatrix}x & + & y\\end{vmatrix}\\le 1)e^{x + y}dA$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186129616 1074 "First, let's draw a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/4f/4f4e38950d36bf77ce4b2a907f07b3f9c4b40837729ef644f37ecfbd921aec8a.png)\n\n  \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b8075ab5905ada88b2a198f73738b2c0c507371cfea32b560c02be4f0185c92f.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-x-1\\le y\\le x+1\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -x+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{|x|+|y|\\le 1}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}{e}^{x+y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-x-1}^{x+1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-x+1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{e}^{x+y}]_{-x-1}^{x+1}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{e}^{x+y}]_{x-1}^{-x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({e}^{2x+1}-{e}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(e-{e}^{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x+1}}{2}-{e}^{-1}x]}_{-1}^{0}+{[ex-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{e}{2}-(\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}+{e}^{-1})]+[(e-\\frac{e}{2})+\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}] \\\\  & =e-{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129653 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186129653 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\cos (y^{3} + π)dydx$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186129653 1074 "![](images/19/19d2d5698ad6b22c003b44d1a023c56ae7779a6d26345732bace3723062f6e0d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/81/81596115241d43f3935ba9e055a3c748c1c1ae283aa2a6a372492ee8c68170cd.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8+\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8+\\pi )}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186130327 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to compute the tangent plane to the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k,$ where $k$ is a constant, at some point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0).$ \n\nAs an example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2 +y^2+z.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that we wish to find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=  (1,1, 2).$ \n\nEvaluating $f$ at the point $(1,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = (1)^2 +(1)^2+2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of our level surface is\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at $(1,1,2),$ we can use the following result:\n\n  *Given a hypersurface $w = f(x,y,z)$ and a point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0,y_0,z_0)$ that lies in the domain of $f$, the gradient $\\nabla \\! f (\\mathbf x_0)$ is normal to the tangent plane of the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k$ at the point $\\mathbf x_0$*. \n\n![](images/11/1140e80fbb2efd684311cb7625e5b0be361d6fb951036253f57b9e51234de3ee.png)\n\n       \n\nWe can now find the equation of the tangent plane to our level surface.\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf x_0 = (1,1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2) & =2(1)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+2\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,  we write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\nabla f(1,1,2) \\cdot \\langle x-1, \\: y-1, \\: z-2 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\langle 2,\\:2,\\:1\\rangle\\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1, z-2\\rangle &= 0\\[3pt]\n      2(x-1)+ 2(y-1) + (z-2) & =0\\\n      2x +2y +z -6&=0   \n}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186130327 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to compute the tangent plane to the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k,$ where $k$ is a constant, at some point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0, y_0, z_0).$ \n\nAs an example, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2 +y^2+z.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that we wish to find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=  (1,1, 2).$ \n\nEvaluating $f$ at the point $(1,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = (1)^2 +(1)^2+2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of our level surface is\n\n$$\nx^2 +y^2+z = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the equation of the tangent plane to this surface at $(1,1,2),$ we can use the following result:\n\n> *Given a hypersurface $w = f(x,y,z)$ and a point $\\mathbf x_0 = (x_0,y_0,z_0)$ that lies in the domain of $f$, the gradient $\\nabla \\! f (\\mathbf x_0)$ is normal to the tangent plane of the level surface $f (x, y, z) = k$ at the point $\\mathbf x_0$*.\n\n![](images/11/1140e80fbb2efd684311cb7625e5b0be361d6fb951036253f57b9e51234de3ee.png)\n\nWe can now find the equation of the tangent plane to our level surface.\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $\\mathbf x_0 = (1,1,2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2) & =2(1)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =2\\text{ }i+2\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally,  we write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\nabla f(1,1,2) \\cdot \\langle x-1, \\: y-1, \\: z-2 \\rangle & = 0 \\[3pt]\n\\langle 2,\\:2,\\:1\\rangle\\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1, z-2\\rangle &= 0\\[3pt]\n      2(x-1)+ 2(y-1) + (z-2) & =0\\\n      2x +2y +z -6&=0   \n}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186130400 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the double integral  \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 2(x+y)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0, \\, y=1, \\, y=2, \\, y=3-x,$ and $y=x.$ How do we proceed?\n\nTo evaluate an integral like this, we carry out the following steps: \n\n**Step 1.** Sketch the region $D$. This allows us to determine the precise set-up of our integral. \n\nIn this example, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0a/0a90a4f377327f5293308f1e08ac6596f1c27b9bd47a614d6d7cb1c5dbfec33c.png)\n\n**Step 2.** Split (if necessary) the region $D$ into several type I or II regions.\n\nFrom the diagram above, we see that our region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions $D_1$ and $D_2,$ as follows.\n\n![](images/a9/a93e1a0a0a7f9c104cb5d380b2a7ff98d6712ef0d2d67b74130da5dcf1701443.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 1, \\quad 1 \\leq y \\leq 2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2= \\{(x,y)\\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3/2,  \\quad  x \\leq y \\leq 3-x\\}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Set up and evaluate the integral using the additivity property of double integrals. We recall the following fact:\n\n*If $D$ is the union of two sub-regions, $D_1, D_2$, that don't overlap then,*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(x+y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}{\\int }_{x}^{3-x}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186130400 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the double integral  \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 2(x+y)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0, \\, y=1, \\, y=2, \\, y=3-x,$ and $y=x.$ How do we proceed?\n\nTo evaluate an integral like this, we carry out the following steps: \n\n**Step 1.** Sketch the region $D$. This allows us to determine the precise set-up of our integral. \n\nIn this example, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0a/0a90a4f377327f5293308f1e08ac6596f1c27b9bd47a614d6d7cb1c5dbfec33c.png)\n\n**Step 2.** Split (if necessary) the region $D$ into several type I or II regions.\n\nFrom the diagram above, we see that our region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions $D_1$ and $D_2,$ as follows.\n\n![](images/a9/a93e1a0a0a7f9c104cb5d380b2a7ff98d6712ef0d2d67b74130da5dcf1701443.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 1, \\quad 1 \\leq y \\leq 2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2= \\{(x,y)\\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3/2,  \\quad  x \\leq y \\leq 3-x\\}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Set up and evaluate the integral using the additivity property of double integrals. We recall the following fact:\n\n> *If $D$ is the union of two sub-regions, $D_1, D_2$, that don't overlap then,*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{D_1}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A + \\iint\\limits_{D_2}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A.\n> $$\n\nSo, finally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(x+y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}{\\int }_{x}^{3-x}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131876 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131877 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131878 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131879 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131880 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475495 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475713 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475735 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475751 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186131875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475776 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186131875 1078 #edb/ref 17592186475776 13194139535148 