n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186133513 1074 "To work out $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3,$ we multiply $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ by itself and then multiply the result by $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133514 1071 "What is the cube of $(-0.5)$?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186133514 1071 "What is the cube of $(-0.5)?$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133514 1074 "To work out\n\n$$\n\\left(-0.5\\right)^3\n$$\n\nwe multiply $-0.5$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-0.5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-0.5)^{3} &= (-0.5) \\cdot (-0.5)^(⏞)^(0.25) \\cdot (-0.5) \\\\\n&= 0.25 \\cdot (-0.5) \\\\\n&=-0.125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186133514 1074 "To work out $\\left(-0.5\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-0.5$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-0.5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-0.5)^{3} & =\\overset{0.25}{\\overset{⏞}{(-0.5)\\cdot (-0.5)}}\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =-0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475924 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475985 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475917 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475936 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475957 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475977 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475961 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475972 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475958 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475968 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475949 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475963 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475919 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475953 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186134021 1050 1080.0 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186134039 1050 420.0 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186134306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186134306 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y = 2x$ and $y = x^{2}$ above the line $y = 2$. What is $∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186134306 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ above the line $y=2.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186134306 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/4e/4e58d7969f0b31206ae8c3385a857c0ad6cf8a42ee79909d51c3a6b58a090852.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=2,$ the $y$-limits are \n$2\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/16/16944d6651297f1c13c39ddf3d9160d721f5f29bfa6b0acac0db46c88295964e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186135183 1064 "To **cube** a number means to multiply that number by itself and then multiply the result by the original number once more.\n\nFor example, to \"cube $2$\", we need to multiply $2$ by $2$ to make $4$, and then multiply this result by $2$ once more. We write the \"cube of $2$\" as $2^3$, so we have\n\n$$\n2^3 = \\underbrace{2\\cdot 2}_{\\large 4} \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the cube of $2$ is $8$\". We could also say \"$2$ cubed equals $8$\".\n\nThe number ${\\color{blue}3}$ in the expression $2^{\\color{blue}3}$ is called the power (or exponent).\n\nThe act of cubing a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $2$ with the following collection of two items:\n\n![](images/b6/b6a13017dcbcf25a66ab576189690e8879a4ccbb3c4c5562b129f4dd15af4e85.png)\n\nThen we can picture $2^3$ by creating a bigger shape (called a **cube**) with length, width and height all equal to $2$:\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)\n\nThere are $8$ items in total in our big cube (one little cube is hiding behind the others), indicating that $2^3=8$.\n\nClearly,\n\n$$\n1^3=1\\cdot1\\cdot1={\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nThe next few first basic cubes are shown below:\n\n| $2^3=2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}8}$ | $3^3=3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}27}$ | $4^3=4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}64}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| ![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png) | ![](images/cb/cb3171dc7f7a2e9838c3e8b46c3ce385be28624b4474391b9bb211b5a599fa55.png) | ![](images/65/6546ffc5df407b29df601252e1f5afa8e01b17dca5c7169a6321d550d86255c3.png) |\n\nThe cubes of our usual counting numbers are called **cube numbers** or **perfect cubes**. We can write them as follows:\n$1^3, 2^3, 3^3, 4^3, 5^3, 6^3, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}8}, {\\color{blue}27}, {\\color{blue}64}, {\\color{blue}125}, {\\color{blue}216}, \\ldots$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186135183 1064 "To **cube** a number means to multiply that number by itself and then multiply the result by the original number once more.\n\nFor example, to \"cube $2$\", we need to multiply $2$ by $2$ to make $4,$ and then multiply this result by $2$ once more. We write the \"cube of $2$\" as $2^3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n2^3 = \\underbrace{2\\cdot 2}_{\\large 4} \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the cube of $2$ is $8$\". We could also say \"$2$ cubed equals $8$\".\n\nThe number ${\\color{blue}3}$ in the expression $2^{\\color{blue}3}$ is called the power (or exponent).\n\nThe act of cubing a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $2$ with the following collection of two items:\n\n![](images/b6/b6a13017dcbcf25a66ab576189690e8879a4ccbb3c4c5562b129f4dd15af4e85.png)\n\nThen we can picture $2^3$ by creating a bigger shape (called a **cube**) with length, width and height all equal to $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)\n\nThere are $8$ items in total in our big cube (one little cube is hiding behind the others), indicating that $2^3=8.$\n\nClearly, $1^3=1\\cdot1\\cdot1={\\color{blue}1}.$ The next few first basic cubes are shown below:\n\n| $2^3=2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}8}$ | $3^3=3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}27}$ | $4^3=4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}64}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png) | ![](images/cb/cb3171dc7f7a2e9838c3e8b46c3ce385be28624b4474391b9bb211b5a599fa55.png) | ![](images/65/6546ffc5df407b29df601252e1f5afa8e01b17dca5c7169a6321d550d86255c3.png) |\n\nThe cubes of our usual counting numbers are called **cube numbers** or **perfect cubes**. We can write them as follows:\n\n$$\n1^3, 2^3, 3^3, 4^3, 5^3, 6^3, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}8}, {\\color{blue}27}, {\\color{blue}64}, {\\color{blue}125}, {\\color{blue}216}, \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186135184 1064 "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n(-)\\cdot(-)\\cdot(-) = (-)\\cdot(-)_(⏟)_((+))\\cdot(-) = (+)\\cdot(-) = (-)\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186135184 1064 "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,{{(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)}_{⏟}}_{(+)}\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(-)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186185730 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185731 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186185730 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185732 13194139535148 175921860