417 false] [17592186188087 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n11-5a + 7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11-5a+7 & = \\\\ (11+7)-5a & = \\\\ 18-5a & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188088 1071 "Simplify the expression $-9v-4(3+5v)$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186188088 1071 "Simplify the expression $-9v-4(3+5v).$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188088 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $-9v$ must be expanded using the distributive law.\n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[4(3+5v)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[12+20v\\Big]\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - 12 - 20v\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-9v - 12 - 20v &=  \\\\\n(-9v - 20v) - 12 &=  \\\\\n&= - 29v - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186188088 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $-9v$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[4(3+5v)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[12+20v\\Big].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - 12 - 20v.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9v-12-20v & = \\\\ (-9v-20v)-12 & = \\\\ -29v-12 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188089 1071 "Simplify the expression $10 - 2(3x - 4)$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186188089 1071 "Simplify the expression $10 - 2(3x - 4).$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188089 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $10$ must be expanded using the distributive law.\n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[2(3x-4)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[6x-8\\Big]\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - 6x + 8\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 - 6x + 8 &=  \\\\\n(10 + 8) - 6x &=  \\\\\n&= 18 - 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186188089 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $10$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[2(3x-4)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[6x-8\\Big].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - 6x + 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-6x+8 & = \\\\ (10+8)-6x & = \\\\ 18-6x & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475944 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475982 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475951 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475946 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475983 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475950 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475981 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188467 1050 420.0 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188575 1071 "Simplify the expression $4 - (2h - 3)$." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186188575 1071 "Simplify the expression $4-(2h-3).$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186188575 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n4-2h+3\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2h+3 & = \\\\ (4+3)-2h & = \\\\ 7-2h & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186189440 1064 "Let's think about how to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n6x-(3+2x)\n$$\n\nIn this example, we're subtracting the entire group of terms in parentheses from $6x$. In such cases, we distribute the negative sign to each term in the parentheses *first* and then simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nRemoving the parentheses by distributing the negative sign, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - (3 + 2x) &=  \\\\\n&= 6x - 3 - 2x \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 3 - 2x &=  \\\\\n(6x - 2x) - 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 4x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's take a look at another example." 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186189440 1064 "Let's think about how to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n6x-(3+2x)\n$$\n\nIn this example, we're subtracting the entire group of terms in parentheses from $6x.$ In such cases, we distribute the negative sign to each term in the parentheses *first* and then simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nRemoving the parentheses by distributing the negative sign, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-(3+2x) & = \\\\ 6x-3-2x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3-2x & = \\\\ (6x-2x)-3 & = \\\\ 4x-3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's take a look at another example." 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186189441 1064 "Sometimes, to subtract a group of terms, we have to apply the distributive law.\n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n7-4(y+2)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n7 - \\Big[4(y+2)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n7 - \\Big[4y+8\\Big]\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as before. Removing the brackets by distributing the negative sign gives\n\n$$\n7 - 4y - 8\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4y - 8 &=  \\\\\n(7 - 8) - 4y &=  \\\\\n&= - 1 - 4y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186189441 1064 "Sometimes, to subtract a group of terms, we have to apply the distributive law.\n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n7-4(y+2)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n7 - \\Big[4(y+2)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n7 - \\Big[4y+8\\Big].\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as before. Removing the brackets by distributing the negative sign gives\n\n$$\n7 - 4y - 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-4y-8 & = \\\\ (7-8)-4y & = \\\\ -1-4y & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186198390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186475984 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186268857 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/01/019f0f9f83ddf323d543d109677335cb3ae72a200baf834453a0eca16a9353ba.png)\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d222e86f6af7c3ed2b35711114c9062d6f0c9b0f2551aa44cac33318c6e5813f.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,x+2\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2