$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ba/ba9c9132c8efdb811e63bebc05e1f46bc8cbc54bcb8246072013a75d53bcb4df.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-y-2}^{0}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-2}^{0}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x-2}^{x+2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186388300 1074 "![](images/e6/e6b8889f9e8025cfca1ee0ae61b67a2f7ec9ec4aee15fab78d2f418a655bbcb8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}-1\\le x\\le 1-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/52/52640b30ded8bf2cd19fe7860c7640bde3c8427ae8978f32fb8020b2f7215468.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=1-x.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x+1}\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-1}^{1-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186388300 1074 "![](images/e6/e6b8889f9e8025cfca1ee0ae61b67a2f7ec9ec4aee15fab78d2f418a655bbcb8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}-1\\le x\\le 1-y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/52/52640b30ded8bf2cd19fe7860c7640bde3c8427ae8978f32fb8020b2f7215468.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=1-x.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-1}^{1-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186394366 1074 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x-2}+1 & \\,⟹\\,x=(y-1)^{2}+2 \\\\ y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x & \\,⟹\\,x=4-2y \\\\ y=8-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=4-\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/3f/3f8e3ccfeac236d7fe9a502f599c61c404607a55ae809ec142019a3f881e1568.png)\n\n  \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/31/3148cb0f499564db9cbfd6b635fef81dc47298789241af4a243c32dbf4dc8247.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,4-2y\\le x\\le 4-\\frac{1}{2}y\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,(y-1)^{2}+2\\le x\\le 4-\\frac{1}{2}y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-2y}^{4-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-1)^{2}+2}^{4-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186394366 1074 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x-2}+1 & \\,⟹\\,x=(y-1)^{2}+2 \\\\ y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x & \\,⟹\\,x=4-2y \\\\ y=8-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=4-\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/3f/3f8e3ccfeac236d7fe9a502f599c61c404607a55ae809ec142019a3f881e1568.png)\n\n  \n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/31/3148cb0f499564db9cbfd6b635fef81dc47298789241af4a243c32dbf4dc8247.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,4-2y\\le x\\le 4-\\frac{1}{2}y}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,(y-1)^{2}+2\\le x\\le 4-\\frac{1}{2}y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-2y}^{4-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-1)^{2}+2}^{4-y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186399622 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\{\\begin{aligned}x=6+2t \\\\ y=5+t \\\\ z=-3-3t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:5x+3y-4z=-43.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186399622 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=6+2t \\\\ y=5+t \\\\ z=-3-3t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:5x+3y-4z=-43.\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186399622 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3y-4z & =-43 \\\\ 5(6+2t)+3(5+t)-4(-3-3t) & =-43 \\\\ 30+10t+15+3t+12+12t & =-43 \\\\ 25t & =-100 \\\\ t & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-4$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=6+2(-4) \\\\ y=5+(-4) \\\\ z=-3-3(-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(-2,1,9).$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186399622 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3y-4z & =-43 \\\\ 5(6+2t)+3(5+t)-4(-3-3t) & =-43 \\\\ 30+10t+15+3t+12+12t & =-43 \\\\ 25t & =-100 \\\\ t & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-4$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=6+2(-4) \\\\ y=5+(-4) \\\\ z=-3-3(-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(-2,1,9).$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186407920 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/27/2722224678aeb1f9ea64f42de2dc4fbf6e139350c291de16500957575fd23850.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 8-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186407920 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/27/2722224678aeb1f9ea64f42de2dc4fbf6e139350c291de16500957575fd23850.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 8-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186407922 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ y=2-x & \\,⟹\\,x=2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =2-y \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =(2-y)^{2} \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =4-4y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/f8/f8fedf15e26f64fc344bd7db6ffaa659bd7c130f36f4a39389b6108136836104.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,2-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-(2-y)^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy & ={[{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186407922 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ y=2-x & \\,⟹\\,x=2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =2-y \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =(2-y)^{2} \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =4-4y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/f8/f8fedf15e26f64fc344bd7db6ffaa659bd7c130f36f4a39389b6108136836104.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,2-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-(2-y)^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy & ={[{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535148 17592186045417 false] [17592186475439 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475987 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475989 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475993 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475999 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1043 #edb/ref 17592186476000 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1045 "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1046 #edb/ref 17592186475999 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049315 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475439 1053 #uuid "41a71fe0-482d-55e4-85d0-de94d279d70a" 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475440 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475988 13194139535148 17592186045417 true] [17592186475440 1043 #edb/ref 17592186475990 13194139535148 